{"id":"native:python-str-dunder-methods","year":"2026","month":"11","slug":"python-str-dunder-methods","path":"/courses/python-for-beginners/python-str-dunder-methods","title":"__str__ and Dunder Methods: Make Your Objects Print Properly","published":"2026-11-26T09:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-11-26T09:00:00.000Z","labels":["Python"],"excerpt":"Replace unreadable default output with __str__ and __repr__, and let your objects work with len(), == and + using dunder methods.","readingMinutes":4,"youtubeId":null,"youtubeThumb":false,"coverImage":null,"headings":[{"id":"the-unhelpful-default","text":"The Unhelpful Default","level":2},{"id":"str-decides-what-print-shows","text":"__str__ Decides What print() Shows","level":2},{"id":"repr-is-for-developers","text":"__repr__ Is for Developers","level":2},{"id":"len-makes-len-work","text":"__len__ Makes len() Work","level":2},{"id":"eq-defines-what-equal-means","text":"__eq__ Defines What \"Equal\" Means","level":2},{"id":"add-teaches-a-new-trick","text":"__add__ Teaches + a New Trick","level":2},{"id":"get-the-code","text":"Get the Code","level":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","level":2}],"html":"<p>You build a class, create an object, print it, and get something like <code>&lt;__main__.Point object at 0x7f3a2c1b4d90&gt;</code>. That tells you the type and a memory address, and nothing about the data inside. Meanwhile Python's own types print nicely, add up with <code>+</code> and answer to <code>len()</code>. How do they do it, and can your classes do the same?</p>\n<p><b>Dunder methods are methods whose names begin and end with double underscores, and Python calls them automatically when you use <code>print</code>, <code>len</code>, <code>==</code> or <code>+</code> on your objects.</b> For example, <code>__str__</code> controls printing, <code>__len__</code> controls <code>len()</code> and <code>__eq__</code> controls <code>==</code>. Define them in your class and your objects behave like built-in ones.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-unhelpful-default\">The Unhelpful Default</h2>\n<p>In this small class, <code>__init__</code> stores each object's starting values, and <code>self</code> is the object being built.</p>\n<pre data-role=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><code><span style=\"color:#F97583\">class</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#B392F0\">Point</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">:</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__init__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self, x, y):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> x</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> y</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">p </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">3</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">)</span>\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(p)</span></code></pre>\n<pre data-role=\"output\"><code>&lt;__main__.Point object at 0x7f3a2c1b4d90&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>(The number after <code>at</code> is a memory address and will be different on your machine.) The output is correct but useless: you cannot see the coordinates.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"str-decides-what-print-shows\">__str__ Decides What print() Shows</h2>\n<p>Define a method named <code>__str__</code> that returns the text you want people to see. Why not just write a <code>show()</code> method? Because <code>print()</code>, f-strings and <code>str()</code> all look for <code>__str__</code> automatically, so one definition works everywhere:</p>\n<pre data-role=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><code><span style=\"color:#F97583\">class</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#B392F0\">Point</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">:</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__init__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self, x, y):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> x</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> y</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__str__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">)\"</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">p </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">3</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">)</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(p)</span>\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(</span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"The point is </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">p</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">)</span></code></pre>\n<pre data-role=\"output\"><code>(2, 3)\nThe point is (2, 3)</code></pre>\n<p>Python calls <code>__str__</code> whenever it needs a readable text version of the object: in <code>print()</code>, in f-strings, and in <code>str(p)</code>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"repr-is-for-developers\">__repr__ Is for Developers</h2>\n<p>There is a second method, <code>__repr__</code>, used when Python shows an object inside a list or other collection, and when you type a name into the Python console. Notice what a list of points looks like when only <code>__str__</code> exists:</p>\n<pre data-role=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><code><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">([Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">1</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">), Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">3</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">4</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">)])</span></code></pre>\n<pre data-role=\"output\"><code>[&lt;__main__.Point object at 0x7f3a2c1b4d90&gt;, &lt;__main__.Point object at 0x7f3a2c1b4d90&gt;]</code></pre>\n<p>Even though we defined <code>__str__</code>, the list falls back to the default for each item. (The addresses are different on your machine, and different for each object.) What is the difference between the two methods? <code>__str__</code> is the friendly version for users; <code>__repr__</code> is the precise version for developers, ideally one that looks like the code that would recreate the object:</p>\n<pre data-role=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><code><span style=\"color:#F97583\">class</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#B392F0\">Point</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">:</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__init__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self, x, y):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> x</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> y</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__str__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">)\"</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__repr__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">)\"</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">3</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">))</span>\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">([Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">1</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">), Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">3</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">4</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">)])</span></code></pre>\n<pre data-role=\"output\"><code>(2, 3)\n[Point(1, 2), Point(3, 4)]</code></pre>\n<p>Now <code>print()</code> still uses <code>__str__</code>, while the list shows each item with <code>__repr__</code>. If you only ever write one of the two, make it <code>__repr__</code>, because Python falls back on it when <code>__str__</code> is missing.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"len-makes-len-work\">__len__ Makes len() Work</h2>\n<p>The built-in <code>len()</code> asks an object for its length by calling its <code>__len__</code> method. Provide one, and your own class works with <code>len()</code>:</p>\n<pre data-role=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><code><span style=\"color:#F97583\">class</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#B392F0\">Playlist</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">:</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__init__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self, songs):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.songs </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> songs</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__len__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">len</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.songs)</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">mix </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Playlist([</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"song a\"</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"song b\"</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"song c\"</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">])</span>\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">len</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(mix))</span></code></pre>\n<pre data-role=\"output\"><code>3</code></pre>\n\n<h2 id=\"eq-defines-what-equal-means\">__eq__ Defines What \"Equal\" Means</h2>\n<p>Why are two points with identical coordinates not equal? By default, Python treats two separate objects as equal only if they are the very same object, even when they hold identical data. Dunder methods let you change that:</p>\n<pre data-role=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><code><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">1</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">) </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">==</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">1</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">))</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#F97583\">class</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#B392F0\">Point</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">:</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__init__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self, x, y):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> x</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> y</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__repr__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">)\"</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__eq__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self, other):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">==</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> other.x </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">and</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">==</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> other.y</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">1</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">) </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">==</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">1</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">))</span>\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">1</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">) </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">==</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">5</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">6</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">))</span></code></pre>\n<pre data-role=\"output\"><code>False\nTrue\nFalse</code></pre>\n<p>The first line used the earlier class, which had no <code>__eq__</code>, so two points with the same coordinates were not equal. After defining <code>__eq__</code>, equality compares the coordinates instead; in that method, <code>other</code> is the object on the right of the <code>==</code>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"add-teaches-a-new-trick\">__add__ Teaches + a New Trick</h2>\n<p>Even operators are dunder methods. Writing <code>a + b</code> makes Python call <code>a.__add__(b)</code>:</p>\n<pre data-role=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><code><span style=\"color:#F97583\">class</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#B392F0\">Point</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">:</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__init__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self, x, y):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> x</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">=</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> y</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__repr__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">f</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">\"Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">{self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">}</span><span style=\"color:#9ECBFF\">)\"</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">    </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">def</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">__add__</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(self, other):</span>\n<span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">        </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">return</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.x </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">+</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> other.x, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">self</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">.y </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">+</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> other.y)</span>\n\n<span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">print</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">(Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">1</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">2</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">) </span><span style=\"color:#F97583\">+</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\"> Point(</span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">3</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">, </span><span style=\"color:#79B8FF\">4</span><span style=\"color:#E1E4E8\">))</span></code></pre>\n<pre data-role=\"output\"><code>Point(4, 6)</code></pre>\n<p>There are many more dunder methods, such as <code>__lt__</code> for sorting with <code>&lt;</code> and <code>__getitem__</code> for square-bracket access. You do not need them all; reach for the one that makes your class feel natural to use.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"get-the-code\">Get the Code</h2>\n<p>Every example in this lesson, with its output, is in a notebook on GitHub: <a href=\"https://github.com/Pradeep-Kumar-Panga/DecodingComplexities/blob/main/Content/PythonCourse/PythonStrDunderMethods/notebook/PythonStrDunderMethods.ipynb\">PythonStrDunderMethods.ipynb</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Dunder methods (double underscore on both sides) are hooks that Python calls for you.</li>\n<li><code>__str__</code> returns the friendly text used by <code>print()</code>, f-strings and <code>str()</code>.</li>\n<li><code>__repr__</code> returns the precise developer text, used inside lists and when no <code>__str__</code> exists.</li>\n<li><code>__len__</code> makes <code>len(obj)</code> work, and <code>__eq__</code> defines what <code>==</code> means.</li>\n<li><code>__add__</code> defines what <code>+</code> does for your objects.</li>\n<li>Without these methods, Python falls back to defaults: addresses for printing, and two objects counting as equal only if they are the very same object.</li>\n</ul>"}